  {%- assign robots_directive = 'noindex, follow' -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- if collection and current_tags == blank -%}
  {%- if collection.products_count == 0 or noindex_handles contains collection.handle -%}
    {%- assign robots_directive = 'noindex, follow' -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- comment -%} Confirmation page: thin, reachable only after a POST, must never rank. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- if page and page.handle == 'newsletter-confirmation' -%}
  {%- assign robots_directive = 'noindex, follow' -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- if page and page.template_suffix == 'pseo' -%}
  {%- unless pseo_indexable_handles contains page.handle -%}
    {%- assign robots_directive = 'noindex, follow' -%}
  {%- endunless -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- comment -%}
  max-image-preview:large added 2026-08-23.

  Audited across the priority pages: only templates/index.liquid and
  templates/page.vault.liquid carried this directive, and both bypass this
  layout with {% layout none %}. Everything routed through here — EVERY product
  page, EVERY collection, EVERY SEO page — emitted a bare "index, follow" and so
  inherited Google's default of max-image-preview:standard, a small thumbnail.

  This store sells one-of-one second-hand pieces and its whole promise is "real
  photos of the exact item". The photograph IS the product. Large image previews
  are the difference between a thumbnail and a picture someone can actually
  judge a garment from, in image results and in AI surfaces that show images.

  Only appended when the page is indexable — on a noindex page an image-preview
  hint has nothing to apply to. Google's docs treat these as independent
  directives on the same tag, so order does not matter.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if robots_directive contains 'noindex' -%}
  <meta name="robots" content="{{ robots_directive }}">
{%- else -%}
  <meta name="robots" content="{{ robots_directive }}, max-image-preview:large">
{%- endif -%}

{% comment %}
  NO HREFLANG — deliberate, verified 2026-08-05.

  hreflang describes a set of EQUIVALENT pages at DIFFERENT URLs. This store
  has exactly one URL per resource. Checked via the Admin API:
      shopLocales      → [{ locale: "en", primary: true, published: true }]
      Market "Morocco" → webPresences: []
  There is no /fr/ or /en/ tree, no alternate-locale domains, no translated
  URLs. Emitting hreflang here would either self-reference (no effect, noise)
  or point at URLs that do not exist (a real error in Search Console).

  The French and English copy on this site is ADDITIONAL CONTENT ON THE SAME
  PAGE, not a translation at another address, so the correct markup for it is
  per-element lang attributes — which lbass-entity-block and lbass-brand-proof
  both use — not hreflang.

  html lang="ar-MA" is correct and should stay: it matches the dominant visible
  language. Note it deliberately disagrees with Shopify's internal locale (en),
  which only governs Shopify's own translation system — unused here.

  Revisit ONLY if real alternate URLs are ever published. Then every one must be
  indexable, self-canonical, and reciprocally linked, or hreflang is ignored.
{% endcomment %}

